  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 238 PM CDT SUN SEP 6 2026\n\nScattered to numerous storms have developed this afternoon within\na very unstable environment as temperatures have easily risen \ninto the mid and upper 90s. The 12z KBMX RAOB this morning \nmeasured steeper lapse rates aloft than we had 24 hours ago, as \nmid to upper level flow has transitioned from easterly to mostly \nnortherly with the upper ridge centered over the southern Plains. \nStorms will continue to develop this afternoon and evening ahead \nof a surface front that is currently moving southward from \nTennessee and northern Georgia. Microburst-type wind gusts will \nbe the primary hazard today, with DCAPE values between 900 and \n1200 J/kg and PWATs between 1.9 and 2.1 inches. Localized gusts of\n40 to 60mph are possible underneath collapsing updrafts that are \nable to mix with the drier air aloft. PoPs this afternoon were \nincreased overall above NBM guidance and may have to be increased \nfurther before all is said and done through the evening hours due \nto additional storm development from outflow boundary collisions \nand intersections.\n\nDangerous heat will continue through the day today as heat\nindices rise as high as 109 degrees mainly along and west of I-\n65.  A Heat Advisory will remain in effect through this evening. \nLooking into the day on Monday, we'll have a slight drop in\ntemperatures and dewpoints overall as surface flow becomes\neasterly. In addition, drier air aloft will advect in from the\nnorth and will limit convective potential across the northern\nhalf of the CWA, while scattered storms will remain in the\nforecast south of I-20. Surface dewpoints are forecast to mix\ntomorrow afternoon into the upper 60s to lower 70s which will\nhelp to keep heat indices mostly below Heat Advisory criteria. At\nthis time, we've opted to not go with a heat advisory for Monday\nbut heat indices will still rise between 100 and 105 for most\nlocations tomorrow afternoon and highs ranging from the upper 90s\nwest to near 90 in the east.  The easterly surface flow is\nexpected to strengthen by Monday afternoon as the pressure\ngradient tightens due to the surface ridge centered over the\nAppalachians.  Wind gusts near 20mph wouldn't be out of the\nquestion, which will certainly provide some much needed heat\nrelief for our eastern and northeastern counties.\n\nThe 500mb ridge is expected to amplify to near 598 decameters and\nbuild eastward by the middle of the upcoming week.  The upper\nlevel height rises and drier air aloft will limit rain chances to\nmainly the I-85 corridor and points south both on Tuesday and\nWednesday with easterly to southeasterly surface flow continuing.\nAn upper level low is then advertised by global guidance to move\nwestward over the northern Gulf by Wednesday and Thursday, which\nwill help transport additional tropical moisture northward into\nthe region. Diurnal shower and storm chances are expected to\nincrease as a result with highs topping out in the low to mid 90s\nareawide. By the end of the week, an upper trough is advertised\nto dive southward toward the Great Lakes with a trough axis\nstretching southwest into the Ohio Valley. A surface cold front\nis advertised to move in our direction by Friday and Saturday,\nbut current guidance would indicate a good chance that there\nwouldn't be enough upper support to make it as far south as\nCentral Alabama. We'll see how those trends evolve over the next\nfew days.\n\n56/GDG",
